            <div class="lesson-body">
                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>Lifecycle Methods Overview</h2>
                    <div class="lifecycle-flow">
                        <div class="lifecycle-step">SetParametersAsync</div>
                        <div class="lifecycle-arrow">↓</div>
                        <div class="lifecycle-step">OnInitialized(Async)</div>
                        <div class="lifecycle-arrow">↓</div>
                        <div class="lifecycle-step">OnParametersSet(Async)</div>
                        <div class="lifecycle-arrow">↓</div>
                        <div class="lifecycle-step">OnAfterRender(Async)</div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>OnInitialized / OnInitializedAsync</h2>
                    <p>This example demonstrates lifecycle methods used to initialize a component. Use <code>OnInitializedAsync</code> for asynchronous initial work (e.g., loading data). Use <code>IDisposable</code> to release resources like timers or event subscriptions.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">@@code {
    private List&lt;Product&gt;? products;

    // Called once when component is first created
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        // Synchronous initialization
    }

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Async initialization - load data
        products = await Http.GetFromJsonAsync&lt;List&lt;Product&gt;&gt;("api/products");
    }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>OnParametersSet / OnParametersSetAsync</h2>
                    <p>Use <code>OnParametersSetAsync</code> to react to changes in component parameters (e.g., reloading data when an input parameter changes).</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">@@code {
    [Parameter]
    public int ProductId { get; set; }

    private Product? product;

    // Called when parameters change
    protected override async Task OnParametersSetAsync()
    {
        // Reload data when ProductId changes
        product = await Http.GetFromJsonAsync&lt;Product&gt;($"api/products/{ProductId}");
    }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>OnAfterRender / OnAfterRenderAsync</h2>
                    <p><code>OnAfterRenderAsync</code> runs after the component has rendered; check the <code>firstRender</code> flag for one-time initialization that needs the DOM (e.g., JS interop or focusing an element).</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">@@code {
    private ElementReference inputElement;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (firstRender)
        {
            // DOM is now available - only runs once
            await inputElement.FocusAsync();
            await JSRuntime.InvokeVoidAsync("initializeChart", chartElement);
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
                    <div class="info-box">
                        <div class="info-box-header"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12.01" y2="8"/></svg><span>Important</span></div>
                        <p>Check <code>firstRender</code> to avoid running initialization code multiple times!</p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>ShouldRender</h2>
                    <p>Override <code>ShouldRender</code> to prevent unnecessary re-renders when state updates but UI doesn't need to change; return false to skip rendering.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">@@code {
    private bool shouldRender = true;

    protected override bool ShouldRender()
    {
        // Return false to skip rendering
        return shouldRender;
    }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>IDisposable - Cleanup</h2>
                    <p>Implement <code>IDisposable</code> (or <code>IAsyncDisposable</code>) to clean up timers, event handlers, or other unmanaged resources to avoid leaks.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">@@implements IDisposable

@@code {
    private Timer? timer;

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        timer = new Timer(_ =&gt; InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged), null, 0, 1000);
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        timer?.Dispose();
    }
}

@@* For async disposal *@@
@@implements IAsyncDisposable

@@code {
    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await SomeAsyncCleanup();
    }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <section class="lesson-section">
                    <h2>StateHasChanged</h2>
                    <p>Call <code>StateHasChanged</code> when you need to force a re-render (for example, after external events or timers); use <code>InvokeAsync</code> when called from non-UI threads.</p>
                    <pre><code class="language-csharp">@@code {
    private int count = 0;

    private void HandleExternalEvent()
    {
        count++;
        StateHasChanged(); // Tell Blazor to re-render
    }

    // For async callbacks from non-UI threads
    private async void OnTimerElapsed()
    {
        count++;
        await InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
    }
}</code></pre>
                </section>

                <LessonTakeaways>
                    <LessonTakeaway>OnInitializedAsync is best for loading initial data.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>OnParametersSetAsync runs when parameters change.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>OnAfterRenderAsync has DOM access - check firstRender.</LessonTakeaway>
                    <LessonTakeaway>Implement IDisposable to clean up resources.</LessonTakeaway>
                </LessonTakeaways>
